ECM only accelerates a MACVLAN in private mode; netifd creates macvlan
devices in the kernel default (vepa) mode, which is what multidial's dial
uplinks use. For every packet of such a flow the post-routing hook allocates
a connection, walks the interface heirarchy, fails in
ecm_interface_establish_and_ref() and frees it all again. Skip those flows
up front, before any connection work, counting them as no-offload.

Local to Ironboxplus/ax9000-immortalwrt-build; on top of VIKINGYFY f9b0ce7's
qca-nss-ecm 8c7355b + patches 001-015.

diff --git a/ecm_interface.c b/ecm_interface.c
index ea45325..8656fb6 100644
--- a/ecm_interface.c
+++ b/ecm_interface.c
@@ -3533,6 +3533,18 @@ static bool ecm_interface_macvlan_mode_is_valid(struct net_device *dev)
 			dev->name, mode);
 	return false;
 }
+
+/*
+ * ecm_interface_is_unaccelerable_macvlan()
+ *	True for a MACVLAN that ecm_interface_establish_and_ref() always refuses
+ *	(any mode but private). The post-routing hooks skip such flows up front:
+ *	otherwise every packet allocates a connection, walks the interface
+ *	heirarchy and frees it all again when the walk fails.
+ */
+bool ecm_interface_is_unaccelerable_macvlan(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+	return netif_is_macvlan(dev) && macvlan_get_mode(dev) != MACVLAN_MODE_PRIVATE;
+}
 #endif
 
